#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd153f7-654f-5ed4-b9fe-3b1731a70ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241460} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa1370c-c82b-5fe3-ad6e-a777ec65e824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242039} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e62bf-fa8c-5810-a8eb-8f31624180ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242635} {:answer/id #uuid "c272c385-4c5e-5735-b854-1a68b031613e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242885} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8dd4bf-32b3-55ab-8c67-2219401fd8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54e62bf-fa8c-5810-a8eb-8f31624180ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242635} :answer-field/id #uuid "5497cc27-067a-5c7c-b637-6c6d86e6b1e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10ef8c30-25b6-5a94-b4c4-25e4aa370204" :question/math-academy-id "q-109336" :question/problem "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,2),(2,4)\\}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124640 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfa5b958-c484-573f-8543-6b8b5e363237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378810} {:answer/id #uuid "4d334e72-a550-56d8-aafb-4277b236292c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378811} {:answer/id #uuid "dec08fd5-f180-53f9-a26e-8fd60c016c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-\\pi \\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378812} {:answer/id #uuid "efc28527-d0de-5b59-8702-a8737af4a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378813} {:answer/id #uuid "00ee5e72-7775-5cd6-8b71-9d8a1fb8c4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d334e72-a550-56d8-aafb-4277b236292c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378811} :answer-field/id #uuid "95142d01-c18d-55cb-b4fa-42e081005dc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55a4348e-da3e-51ea-bc50-dd5e0f2b136a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158580" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function  $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡({x}^{2}+y).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The argument of the arcsine has values between $-1$ and $1.$\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab403f1-37e8-5537-b482-1b497f41b6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319614} {:answer/id #uuid "7defa742-522c-5545-9656-199144eab8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319768} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebdd4c7-2b1d-5b85-aec1-f2e0254b78bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319818} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ae9ea2-f7af-5bb3-9254-c194f6f8817e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320039} {:answer/id #uuid "eebdfceb-9ba4-5843-ba91-acb1afc553e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eebdfceb-9ba4-5843-ba91-acb1afc553e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320107} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab218b2-bffd-5950-8dc4-5254fdc0b910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e1095e2-b747-574e-a018-9f92abb5fb55" :question/math-academy-id "q-35299" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{10}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Extending a Set to Form an Orthogonal Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6180973b-2ca2-50de-9a9e-68ad998f74d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324784} {:answer/id #uuid "cabb893b-4474-531c-9b52-e7bfa197b717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325728} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9d258f-269f-5822-ae1d-93b483d1fe47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325757} {:answer/id #uuid "eca5b0f1-ed13-5a6c-9570-cfbdc1e122b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326054} {:answer/id #uuid "ecba0e97-34e3-516d-bbc5-8223fa2b6610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eca5b0f1-ed13-5a6c-9570-cfbdc1e122b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326054} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfce3a6b-16cb-5cbf-9f62-1141d301a3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "455820f5-9352-535f-a3b2-e3fbc55031b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-40297" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above, which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"}]}
